 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Supply",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "A bias network sets the supply current so that it stays the same for any supply from 2 V to 30 V; about 60 µA for all four comparators. A bypass capacitor on the supply is usually needed. The supply input is protected against reverse voltage. Split supplies also work."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Unused comparators",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Every pin of a comparator that is not used goes to ground."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Inputs",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "The inputs are PNP, so the common-mode range includes ground. The difference between the inputs may exceed VCC without harm. Neither input may go more than 0.3 V below ground; protection is needed to keep it from doing so."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output stage",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Each output has two modes. While the output stays above about 1 V, a Darlington pair (Q1 driving Q2) sinks current limited only by the two transistors' hFE, the 6 µA bias current I1 and Q2's 60 Ω saturation resistance: 30 mA at VO = 2 V, enough for LEDs or relays, with supply current still about 60 µA. Below about 0.8 V Q1 would saturate and the sink current collapse, so a third transistor, Q3, steers I1 straight into Q2's base, and the output then sinks roughly I1 × hFE(Q2), 700 µA at VO = 0.4 V. The change between the two modes is smooth."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Open collector",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "The output in both modes is an uncommitted collector. Outputs may be wired together to form a wired-AND (dot) function, and the pull-up may go to any supply within the permitted range, independent of the voltage on VCC."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Compatibility",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The outputs suit MOS and CMOS logic. The pinout matches the LM239, LM339 and LM2901."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Uses",
   "page": 1,
